{
  "fixedHtml": "<input autocomplete=\"bday\" id=\"ubirthday\" type=\"text\">",
  "explanation": "Changed the `autocomplete` attribute for the birthday input from the invalid token 'birthday' to the correct HTML specification token 'bday'. This allows the input's purpose to be programmatically determined, resolving WCAG 1.3.5.",
  "effort": "low"
}

{
  "fixedHtml": "<img src=\"squiggle.gif\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\" alt=\"\">",
  "explanation": "Added an empty `alt` attribute (`alt=\"\"`) to the `<img>` element. This signals to assistive technologies, such as screen readers, that the image is decorative and conveys no meaningful information, thus it should be ignored. This resolves the WCAG 1.1.1 violation by ensuring that non-text content that is purely decorative is properly handled.",
  "effort": "low"
}

{
  "fixedHtml": "<div>\n  Tree type: <img src=\"spacer.gif\" width=\"100\" height=\"1\" alt=\"\">\n  Cedrus deodara\n</div>",
  "explanation": "The `alt` attribute for an image that is purely decorative or used for spacing should be empty (`alt=\"\"`). This signals to screen readers that the image conveys no meaningful information and should be ignored, preventing unnecessary announcements like \"spacer\". This satisfies WCAG 1.1.1 by ensuring non-text content has an appropriate text alternative (in this case, an empty one for decorative content).",
  "effort": "low"
}

{
  "fixedHtml": "<h2 class=\"title\">Big text</h2>",
  "explanation": "Changed the `<p>` element to an `<h2>` element. This makes the section heading programmatically identifiable to assistive technologies, fulfilling WCAG 2.4.10. The `class=\"title\"` was retained to preserve existing visual styling.",
  "effort": "low"
}

{
  "fixedHtml": "<label for=\"email-input\">Email address:</label>\n<input type=\"text\" id=\"email-input\" name=\"email\">",
  "explanation": "A `<label>` element was added with descriptive text 'Email address:' and its `for` attribute was set to 'email-input'. The `<input>` element was given an `id` of 'email-input' to programmatically associate it with the label. This ensures that users, especially those using assistive technologies, understand the purpose of the input field, addressing WCAG 3.3.2.",
  "effort": "low"
}

{
  "fixedHtml": "<h1 class=\"heading1\">Introduction</h1>",
  "explanation": "Changed the `p` element to an `h1` element. This provides the correct semantic markup for a heading, allowing assistive technologies to identify it as such and convey the page structure to users, thereby resolving the WCAG 1.3.1 violation. The CSS class was retained to maintain the visual styling.",
  "effort": "low"
}

{
  "fixedHtml": "<form>\n  <label for=\"firstname\">First name:</label>\n  <input type=\"text\" id=\"firstname\" name=\"firstname\">\n</form>",
  "explanation": "The text \"First name:\" was wrapped in a <label> element, and its 'for' attribute was set to 'firstname'. The <input> element was given an 'id' attribute matching 'firstname'. This explicitly associates the label with the input, providing an accessible name for assistive technologies as required by WCAG 4.1.2.",
  "effort": "low"
}

{
  "fixedHtml": "<p>Interested in learning more? Write to us at</p>\n<p>3333 Third Avenue, Suite 300 &middot; New York City</p>\n<p>And we'll send you the complete informational packet absolutely Free!</p>",
  "explanation": "The `<h4>` element was replaced with a `<p>` element. The content, an address, does not introduce a new section and therefore should not be marked up as a heading. Using a heading for purely visual styling misrepresents the document structure, violating WCAG 1.3.1. Visual styling (e.g., large bold text) should be applied using CSS to the `<p>` element, not by misusing semantic heading tags.",
  "effort": "medium"
}

{
  "fixedHtml": "<a href=\"scores.html\">\n  <img src=\"football.gif\" alt=\"Football Scoreboard\">\n</a>\n<a href=\"scores.html\">Football Scoreboard</a>",
  "explanation": "The `alt` attribute of the image within the first link was updated to \"Football Scoreboard\". This provides a meaningful accessible name for the image link, ensuring that screen reader users can understand its purpose without having to guess from the filename. The text for the `alt` attribute was derived from the adjacent, duplicate text link, which clearly states the link's purpose. This resolves the WCAG 2.4.4 violation by making the link's purpose determinable.",
  "effort": "low"
}

{
  "fixedHtml": "<img src=\"../images/animal.jpg\" alt=\"An animal\">",
  "explanation": "Added an `alt` attribute to the `<img>` element with a descriptive text \"An animal\". This provides a text alternative for users of assistive technologies, fulfilling WCAG 1.1.1 by ensuring the image's purpose can be conveyed.",
  "effort": "low"
}

{
  "fixedHtml": "<p style=\"text-align: left;\">\n How much wood would a woodchuck chuck if a woodchuck could chuck wood?\n He would chuck, he would, as much as he could, and chuck as much wood\n As a woodchuck would if a woodchuck could chuck wood.\n</p>",
  "explanation": "The 'text-align: justify' CSS property, which was causing the issue, was overridden by adding an inline style 'text-align: left;' to the paragraph element. Fully justified text creates uneven word spacing between words, which can significantly hinder readability for users with cognitive disabilities, dyslexia, or low vision. Aligning text to the left (or right for right-to-left languages) ensures consistent word spacing, improving legibility and complying with WCAG 1.4.8 Visual Presentation (No justification).",
  "effort": "low"
}

{
  "fixedHtml": "<p>A British businessman has racked up 2 million flyer miles and plans to travel on the world's first commercial tourism flights to space.</p>\n<p><a href=\"ff.html\" aria-label=\"Read More about the British businessman's space travel plans\">Read More...</a></p>",
  "explanation": "The `aria-label` attribute was added to the `<a>` tag. This attribute provides a descriptive label for assistive technologies, clarifying the purpose of the \"Read More...\" link without changing its visible text. This ensures that the link's purpose can be determined programmatically, satisfying WCAG 2.4.4.",
  "effort": "low"
}
